			<div class="content" id="post-content-2721764">
									

<p>My main critique:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The luminosity increased sharply, and caused irreversible retinal damage to researchers viewing the live feed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This, as written, is patently ridiculous. Computer/video monitors aren't magic, and unless the Foundation is intentionally building monitors with high-powered search lights in them for the express purposes of blinding researchers, the worst that can happen is that the screen goes white and hits its maximum brightness. If you were going for the &quot;mine eyes have seen the glory, no man looks on the face of God&quot; type crap, fine, but that's not what it reads as now, and you should reword it.</p>
<p>Even if that is what you're going for, there's nothing in the rest of the SCP that thematically matches with that ability, so it reads just like random unrelated junk to make this more <em>spooooooky</em>. (Side note: If blinding people with the majesty cognitohazard-style is thematically out in left field, specifically being able to overclock mundane computer monitors isn't even in the tri-county area, let alone the ballpark.)</p>
<p>Overall&#8230; No vote for now, but I'm actively thinking on it. (Edit: On the downside, this is built out some of the oldest tropes on the wiki. It's a weird tunnel system that can't be reached from other angles and is made of human tissue and full of weird worm things. If we're looking for SCPs which meet at least 2 out of those 4, there are <em>dozens</em>.) I still enjoyed the building up of the story in the tunnels, but I feel like I would rather have been left in complete mystery then given what was said in the last addendum.</p>

